  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation.",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}-2x-\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+4-(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+7) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
